# umount.pdxfs — caps.decl
#
# Wave: R53 (design/tooling/volume-tooling-ux.md §2.3 + §5 + §9.3, paideia-os
# repo). umount.pdxfs is a standalone CLI tool (a ring-3 ELF with its own
# `_start`, not a shared library like libpdx-volume/libpdx-audit): it
# performs its own syscalls directly (self-contained inlined-syscall
# pattern, matching src/user/true.pdx / cat.pdx / mkdir.pdx in the
# paideia-os monorepo, and mkfs.pdxfs's own M1 landing in this same repo
# family) rather than delegating every kernel touch to a linked library.
#
# consumed_kinds (kernel-owned; paideia-os repo):
#
#   KIND_USER              = 0x190  (src/kernel/core/cap/kind_user.pdx)
#     -- invoker identity, consumed for audit attribution
#        (`invoker_user` field of PdxFsUnmountRecord@0.1, §5.6). Not
#        dereferenced by any M1 code path -- M1's --dry-run record is
#        the task-scoped 3-field slice (target, flags, result_code);
#        it never performs a KIND_USER lookup. Real invoker_user
#        population lands at umount.pdxfs.M3-003 (semantic-pipe emit
#        of the full 15-field record) alongside audit-journal
#        attribution.
#
#   KIND_PDXFS_MOUNT_TABLE(read+write) = 0x1A5  (R53-PREP-001,
#     src/kernel/core/cap/kind_pdxfs_mount_table.pdx)
#     -- read authority to resolve a `<mount-point>` positional to a
#        mount_id via libpdx-volume.mount_table_snapshot (§5.1,
#        umount.pdxfs.M2-001); write authority to remove the row once
#        the unmount completes (§5.5 step 5, umount.pdxfs.M2-003). Not
#        invoked by any M1 code path -- M1's --dry-run branch never
#        calls PMT_OP_READ_ROW / PMT_OP_LIST / PMT_OP_APPEND_ROW (the
#        kind's own landed op catalog, per
#        src/kernel/core/cap/kind_pdxfs_mount_table.pdx SECTION 6); it
#        only inspects the raw `<mount-id | mount-point>` argv string
#        to render the dry-run line. Real mount-point -> mount_id
#        resolution is umount.pdxfs.M2-001.
#
#   KIND_VOLUME(flush+unmount, <resolved-cap>) = 0x1A0  (R52,
#     src/kernel/core/cap/kind_volume.pdx)
#     -- resolved by looking up the mount-table row (§5.2), not held
#        ambiently at process start. "flush+unmount" is this design
#        doc's own §5.2 rights label; the KIND's REAL landed op
#        catalog (per libpdx-volume's own caps.decl in
#        paideia-satellites/libpdx-volume) is VOL_OP_QUERY_* +
#        VOL_OP_DEBUG_PRINT (ordinals 0..9, VK_OPS_VALID_MASK = 0x3FF)
#        -- there is no landed "unmount" op yet, and §5.3's
#        `VOL_OP_QUERY_HANDLE_COUNT` is itself flagged in
#        volume-tooling-ux.md §5.3 as "a new op added by R53.M2",
#        i.e. not yet landed kernel-side as of this M1 caps.decl. This
#        mirrors the same "design-doc rights label vs. real landed op
#        catalog" gap mkfs.pdxfs's own caps.decl flags for
#        KIND_BLOCK_DEVICE -- confirm the op numbering with osarch
#        before umount.pdxfs.M2-002 (handle-count query) and M2-003
#        (cache-flush + checkpoint path) implement a real cap-invoke
#        against either op. Not invoked by any M1 code path.
#
#   KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL   = 0x191  (R48b, src/kernel/core/cap/
#     kind_elevate_channel.pdx, derived over KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT base 5)
#     -- referenced by volume-tooling-ux.md §5.2 + §8.2 for the
#        `--force` elevate gate (only for mount points under
#        `/system`, `/boot`, `/dev` -- mirrors mount.pdxfs's own §4.2
#        table). Not declared as an actively consumed kind at M1: no
#        code path in this landing calls libpdx-elevate, which itself
#        has not been located under paideia-satellites/ as of this
#        landing (same "design-doc-mentioned, not yet bootstrapped"
#        status mkfs.pdxfs's own caps.decl documents) -- see
#        src/elevate.pdx's module header in this repo and
#        design/architecture.md §3 for the deferral note.
#
# declares_output_schemas: none bound at M1. `PdxFsUnmountRecord@0.1`'s
# FIELD SHAPE is fixed by design/tooling/volume-tooling-ux.md §5.6 (15
# fields: audit_id, mount_id, volume_uuid, mount_point, invoker_user,
# dirty_pages_flushed, txns_drained, journal_state, result_code,
# open_handles, revoked_handles, deferred, ts_ns). This M1 landing's
# --dry-run path prints a line-based textual rendering of exactly the
# three fields this issue's own acceptance shape asks for (target,
# flags, result_code) -- see src/unmount_record.pdx's module header for
# which fields M1 omits and why. The typed schema-registry binding
# (BLAKE3-truncated fingerprint constant, semantic-pipe framing) is
# owned by umount.pdxfs.M3-003 per volume-tooling-ux.md §7.3 + §7.4, not
# this issue.
#
# M1 landing status (see STATUS.md): this repo performs exactly two
# syscalls at M1 -- `sys_write` (SC+ ID 1, to fd 1 for the dry-run
# record and fd 2 for error diagnostics) and `sys_exit` (SC+ ID 60). No
# sys_open, no sys_svc_lookup, no sys_ipc_send, no cap-invoke of any
# kind -- every KIND above is declared for the SHAPE this tool's future
# milestones consume, per the same "typed-against, not yet touched"
# posture mkfs.pdxfs's own M1 caps.decl documents for its stub
# functions.
#
# M2 landing status (see STATUS.md; issues #4/#5/#6): STILL no cap-invoke
# of any kind, and none of the three KIND gaps this file already flagged
# above have closed. Specifics, confirmed by direct kernel/library grep
# during M2:
#
#   - `KIND_PDXFS_MOUNT_TABLE`: no ambient cap-slot convention exists
#     anywhere (design doc or this repo's own argv surface) for THIS
#     tool to obtain a live singleton row, so `src/target_resolve.pdx`'s
#     mount-point-path branch calls `libpdx-volume.mount_table_snapshot`
#     against a deliberately all-zero local row (real call, real link
#     edge, safe-by-construction failure -- see that file's own module
#     header). `PMT_OP_MAX` = 3 (LIST/READ_ROW/APPEND_ROW/QUERY_SLOT_
#     COUNT) also has no remove-row op, so `src/unmount_op.pdx`'s row-
#     removal step (§5.5 step 5) is a documented stub (UO_STUB_ROW_
#     REMOVE).
#   - `KIND_VOLUME`: `VOL_OP_QUERY_HANDLE_COUNT` still has not landed
#     (`kind_volume.pdx`'s `VOL_OP_MAX` is still 9, ops 0..9 only, no new
#     ordinal). A SECOND, independently-blocking gap surfaced at M2: the
#     real `_mount_table[]` per-slot layout carries no cap-slot field at
#     all, so there is no way to derive WHICH `KIND_VOLUME` cap a
#     resolved `mount_id` corresponds to even if the op existed.
#     `src/handle_check.pdx`'s handle-count query is therefore a hard
#     stub (always reports 0 handles); the IN_USE refusal LOGIC is real
#     and live, just structurally unreachable until both gaps close.
#   - `sys_umount` (sysno 76): the kernel BODY landed
#     (`src/kernel/core/syscall/sys_umount.pdx`, R53.M2-003 #1738) but
#     `dispatch.pdx`'s `dispatch_umount` label was never rewired off its
#     original R53.M2-001 (#1736) `-ENOSYS` stub -- confirmed by direct
#     read of dispatch.pdx as of this M2 landing. `src/unmount_op.pdx`
#     issues the real syscall anyway and treats the deterministic
#     `-ENOSYS` as a documented stub condition (bit 0 of its stub_mask),
#     not a hard failure, per this milestone's own "the overall happy
#     path should still complete and emit OK" instruction. **Flagged for
#     main**: this is a one-block dispatch.pdx rewrite (mirror
#     `dispatch_open`'s KPTI-bounce shape), not a design gap.
#
# M3 landing status (see STATUS.md; issues #7/#8/#9/#10): three new
# satellite-library links, no new syscalls of this repo's own, none of
# the three M2 kernel/library gaps above have closed.
#
# consumed_satellite_libs (all cross-repo bare-label `call`s, the same
# mechanism `src/target_resolve.pdx`'s M2 `mount_table_snapshot` call
# already used):
#
#   libpdx-volume (M3-004, #10) -- `pdxb_sign_superblock`
#     (`src/unmount_op.pdx`'s step 4). REAL call, against a deliberately
#     PLACEHOLDER 4096-byte `.bss` scratch buffer (not the genuine
#     on-disk superblock -- this tool still has no block-device read
#     primitive) and a real, non-null, all-zero 32-byte seed buffer
#     (not a real seed -- same "seed is zero, not unavailable" posture
#     mkfs.pdxfs's own M3-004 landing documents for the identical
#     intrinsic). The SIG_KEY_LOCKED refusal gate this call feeds is
#     real and live but unreachable: the `MlDsa65::sign` intrinsic only
#     fails on a NULL `seed_ptr`, and this buffer is never null.
#
#   libpdx-audit (@0.2, commit bfc63a5, landed dependency) --
#     `AuditClient::audit_begin`, `AuditClient::audit_record_output`,
#     `AuditClient::audit_commit` (`src/audit_wire.pdx`, #9). REAL calls
#     (real IPC send attempts), but the daemon-side dispatch is the same
#     confirmed-STUBBED `audit_journal_broker_dispatch` mkfs.pdxfs's own
#     M3-004 landing documents -- every call here may therefore no-op at
#     the daemon, per the same forgiving-posture instruction that
#     landing established. `audit_record_output` is called exactly once
#     per invocation, only on the `--lazy` DEFERRED arm (the "DEFERRED"
#     mid-lifecycle audit emission #9 asks for).
#
#   libpdx-elevate (4844 LOC, mature -- landed dependency) -- NOT
#     actually called for a real elevation decision
#     (`src/elevate.pdx`, #8): this library's real API
#     (`elevate_client_acquire`/`elevate_client_require`) needs a
#     `parent_ep_slot` broker-endpoint cap this repo does not hold
#     (`KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL = 0x191`, still the M1 placeholder,
#     unchanged by this landing). `elevate_request_force_unmount` is
#     therefore a fail-closed stub returning DENY unconditionally --
#     the identical gap and posture mkfs.pdxfs's own M3-005
#     `elevate_wire.pdx` documents over the same library.
#
#   libpdx-semantic-pipe (v1.0.0, real + released) -- NOT linked
#     (`src/pipe_wire.pdx`, #9): confirmed via GitHub issue
#     paideia-os#2000 that `Registry::bind_by_name` is inert (the
#     backing `svc.schema-registry` service does not exist yet). Per
#     mkfs.pdxfs's own M3-003 fallback precedent, every
#     `PdxFsUnmountRecord@0.1` this repo emits remains the M1/M2
#     line-based `sys_write(1, ...)` rendering, now preceded by one
#     documented deferral header line.
#
# New (real, live) refusal/stub result codes added at M3 (see
# `src/unmount_op.pdx` / `src/unmount_record.pdx` for the full bit/code
# tables): `UO_RC_DEFERRED`/`UR_RC_DEFERRED` (4, --lazy, stubbed
# completion), `UO_RC_ELEVATION_STUB`/`UR_RC_ELEVATION_STUB` (5,
# --force, fail-closed), `UO_RC_SIG_KEY_LOCKED`/`UR_RC_SIG_KEY_LOCKED`
# (6, superblock re-sign refusal, real gate, unreachable).
